WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.636
Lesson five.

00:00:01.636 --> 00:00:06.632
Speciation is the part of NEAT that gives new ideas time to work.

00:00:07.232 --> 00:00:08.841
Here is the problem.

00:00:08.841 --> 00:00:15.737
A mutation that adds a node usually makes the network worse at first, because its new weights are not tuned yet.

00:00:15.737 --> 00:00:24.198
If every genome competed against every other one, that network would be thrown out right away, before it ever had a chance to improve.

00:00:24.798 --> 00:00:28.845
So NEAT measures how different two genomes are, using their genes.

00:00:28.845 --> 00:00:31.307
It counts the genes that don't match.

00:00:31.307 --> 00:00:35.354
It adds half of the weight differences of the genes that do match.

00:00:35.354 --> 00:00:38.964
And it divides the total by the size of the larger genome.

00:00:38.964 --> 00:00:43.668
Our library does this for the connections and for the nodes, and adds the two.

00:00:43.668 --> 00:00:49.000
If that distance is below two point two, the two genomes belong to the same species.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.242
Genomes then compete mostly inside their own species.

00:00:53.242 --> 00:01:00.228
Each species shares its offspring among its members, so a big species can't simply crowd out a small new one.

00:01:00.228 --> 00:01:08.290
And a species that stops improving for twenty generations is removed, so the population doesn't waste effort on dead ends.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:12.317
This is the classic NEAT picture, from our run.

00:01:12.317 --> 00:01:18.298
Each band is one species, and its height shows how many of the 150 genomes belong to it.

00:01:18.298 --> 00:01:24.393
At the very start there is just one species, because every genome has the same shape.

00:01:24.993 --> 00:01:27.966
As mutations pile up, new species split off.

00:01:27.966 --> 00:01:32.598
They grow when they do well, and shrink and die when they stop improving.

00:01:32.598 --> 00:01:36.500
Over the whole run, twenty-seven species came and went.
